<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bad Pyrmont</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bad Pyrmont is a spa town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> hills south-west of Hamelin. Its mineral springs made it one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s fashionable spas from the 17th century, visited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> and Goethe. It was the seat of the small county of Pyrmont, ruled with Waldeck from 1625, until it joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bad-pyrmont.jpg" alt="Bad Pyrmont" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Roger Veringmeier · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bad_Pyrmont_Hylligen_Born_Heilige_bron_Holy_source_fountain_Avenue_Hauptallee.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bad Pyrmont stands on the river Emmer, about 10 km west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>, in the district of Hamelin-Pyrmont in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It has a population close to 19,000. It gained its reputation as a fashionable place for princely holidays in the 17th and 18th centuries, and it is the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quakers">Religious Society of Friends</a>, the Quakers, in Germany. Its economy is geared largely to tourism, and Bad Pyrmonter mineral water is bottled in the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town was formerly called simply Pyrmont and was the seat of a small county for much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The county gained its independence from the County of Schwalenberg in 1194 and kept it until the comital line died out in 1494. It then passed by inheritance to Spiegelberg, in 1557 to Lippe and in 1583 to the County of Gleichen.</p><p>In 1625 it became part of the much larger County of Waldeck, also by inheritance. In 1668 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-chamber-court">Imperial Chamber Court</a> rejected the claim of the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> that Pyrmont had been pledged as security for a loan, and confirmed the rights of the Count of Waldeck, who gave the Amt of Lügde, until then the county's capital, to the bishopric in compensation. In January 1712 Emperor Charles VI raised the Count of Waldeck and Pyrmont, who had combined the two titles the previous year, to hereditary prince.</p><p>From 1805 to 1812 Pyrmont was again a separate principality, after the death of the previous prince. The two parts were reunited in 1812, and Waldeck-Pyrmont kept its status after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 and joined the German Confederation. The people of Pyrmont protested in 1813 at their lack of autonomy, and until a formal union in 1849 the two territories remained constitutionally separate.</p><p>From 1868 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> administered the principality, under a ten-year contract that was repeatedly renewed, and in 1871 it became a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>. The prince abdicated during the German Revolution at the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, and after a local plebiscite the town and district of Pyrmont were detached and incorporated into the Prussian Province of Hanover.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The spa has a large Kurpark with an outdoor palm garden. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> castle, built in 1706–10, is part of a substantial complex of fortifications dating from the 16th century and now houses the Museum of Municipal and Spa History. The physicist Max Born, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> winner, spent his last years in the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bad Pyrmont” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It joined Prussia's Province of Hanover in 1922 and is now <b>Bad Pyrmont</b> in Lower Saxony.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-pyrmont">County of Pyrmont</a></li></ul></section>
